    "markdown": "---\r\ntitle: Coordinate Geometry\r\naudience: CBSE Class 10\r\nsubject: Mathematics\r\nchapter_number: 7\r\nchapter_slug: coordinate-geometry\r\nstatus: draft\r\ncontent_type: chapter-pack\r\ncurriculum_scope_note: Coordinate-triangle area and collinearity are support-only,\r\n  not 2026-27 board topics\r\ntype: ChapterPack\r\ndescription: Class 10 CBSE notes for Coordinate Geometry.\r\ntags:\r\n- cbse\r\n- class-10\r\n- coordinate-geometry\r\ntimestamp: '2026-07-08T12:00:00Z'\r\n---\r\n# Coordinate Geometry\r\n\r\n> 2026-27 curriculum scope: the distance formula and internal section formula are board-exam topics. Coordinate-triangle area and area-based collinearity remain support material and must not be counted as board coverage.\r\n\r\nThis is an original teaching draft for Class 10 Mathematics, Chapter 7. The chapter becomes easier when students connect geometry to point positions on the plane.\r\n\r\n## chapter goal\r\n\r\nBy the end of this chapter, a student should be able to:\r\n- locate points on the Cartesian plane\r\n- use the distance formula\r\n- use the section formula\r\n- find the midpoint of a line segment\r\n- recognise coordinate-triangle area as a support method outside current board scope\r\n\r\n## teacher note on textbook alignment\r\n\r\nThe current NCERT Class 10 chapter strongly focuses on:\r\n- distance between two points\r\n- internal division of a line segment\r\n- midpoint as a special case of section formula\r\n- area of a triangle from coordinates (support-only)\r\n\r\n## chapter flow for the platform\r\n\r\n1. Cartesian plane refresher\r\n2. Distance formula\r\n3. Section formula\r\n4. Midpoint formula\r\n5. Area of triangle\r\n6. Collinearity using area\r\n7. Quick revision and practice\r\n\r\n## 1) Cartesian plane refresher\r\n\r\nA point is written as `(x, y)`.\r\n\r\n- `x` tells horizontal position\r\n- `y` tells vertical position\r\n\r\nThe plane has four quadrants.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n`(3, 2)` lies in the first quadrant.\r\n\r\n## 2) distance formula\r\n\r\nIf the points are `(x1, y1)` and `(x2, y2)`, then distance is:\r\n\r\n`d = sqrt((x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2)`\r\n\r\nThis comes from the Pythagoras theorem.\r\n\r\n### solved example\r\nFind the distance between `(2, 3)` and `(8, 11)`.\r\n\r\n`d = sqrt((8 - 2)^2 + (11 - 3)^2)`\r\n\r\n`= sqrt(6^2 + 8^2)`\r\n\r\n`= sqrt(36 + 64)`\r\n\r\n`= sqrt(100) = 10`\r\n\r\n## 3) section formula\r\n\r\nIf point `P(x, y)` divides the line joining `A(x1, y1)` and `B(x2, y2)` internally in the ratio `m:n`, then:\r\n\r\n`x = (mx2 + nx1)/(m + n)`\r\n\r\n`y = (my2 + ny1)/(m + n)`\r\n\r\n### meaning\r\nThe coordinates of the dividing point are weighted averages of the end points.\r\n\r\n### warning\r\nThe ratio `m:n` means:\r\n`AP:PB = m:n`\r\n\r\nStudents often reverse this.\r\n\r\n## 4) midpoint formula\r\n\r\nMidpoint is the special case where ratio is `1:1`.\r\n\r\nSo midpoint of `(x1, y1)` and `(x2, y2)` is:\r\n\r\n`((x1 + x2)/2, (y1 + y2)/2)`\r\n\r\n### example\r\nMidpoint of `(4, 6)` and `(10, 2)` is:\r\n\r\n`((4 + 10)/2, (6 + 2)/2) = (7, 4)`\r\n\r\n## 5) area of a triangle\r\n\r\nIf the vertices are:\r\n- `(x1, y1)`\r\n- `(x2, y2)`\r\n- `(x3, y3)`\r\n\r\nthen area is:\r\n\r\n`1/2 [x1(y2 - y3) + x2(y3 - y1) + x3(y1 - y2)]`\r\n\r\nTake the absolute value if needed, because area cannot be negative.\r\n\r\n### important use\r\nIf the area of a triangle is zero, the three points are collinear.\r\n\r\n## 6) collinearity using area\r\n\r\nThree points are collinear if they lie on the same straight line.\r\n\r\nUsing the coordinate formula:\r\n- if area = `0`, points are collinear\r\n- if area != `0`, points are not collinear\r\n\r\nThis is a common short-answer and proof-based application.\r\n\r\n## 7) exam-friendly summary notes\r\n\r\n### must-know facts\r\n- Distance formula comes from Pythagoras theorem.\r\n- Section formula gives coordinates of a dividing point.\r\n- Midpoint formula is a special case of section formula.\r\n- Area formula helps test collinearity.\r\n- Area must be taken as positive finally.\r\n\r\n### one-line revision points\r\n- distance uses square root\r\n- midpoint means average\r\n- area zero means same line\r\n\r\n## 8) common mistakes students make\r\n\r\n1. Subtracting coordinates in the wrong order and then squaring wrongly.\r\n2. Reversing the ratio in the section formula.\r\n3. Forgetting that midpoint is just average of coordinates.\r\n4. Leaving area negative.\r\n5. Forgetting to divide by `2` in the area formula.\r\n\r\n## 9) board tips\r\n\r\n- Write the coordinate pairs clearly before substituting.\r\n- Use brackets carefully when numbers are negative.\r\n- In section-formula questions, state the ratio and the two end points first.\r\n- In collinearity questions, write the area step fully and conclude with a sentence.\r\n\r\n## 10) how to remember the chapter\r\n\r\n### memory box 1\r\nDistance formula is just Pythagoras on the graph.\r\n\r\n### memory box 2\r\nMidpoint means average of x-values and average of y-values.\r\n\r\n### memory box 3\r\nArea zero means the triangle has collapsed into a line.\r\n\r\n## 11) solved practice set\r\n\r\n### question 1\r\nFind the distance between `(1, 1)` and `(4, 5)`.\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\n`d = sqrt((4 - 1)^2 + (5 - 1)^2)`\r\n`= sqrt(9 + 16) = 5`\r\n\r\n### question 2\r\nFind the midpoint of `(2, -1)` and `(8, 7)`.\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\n`((2 + 8)/2, (-1 + 7)/2) = (5, 3)`\r\n\r\n### question 3\r\nWhat can you say if the area formed by three points is zero?\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\nThe three points are collinear.\r\n\r\n## 12) short answer practice\r\n\r\n1. Why is the distance formula related to Pythagoras theorem?\r\n2. What does section formula find?\r\n3. How is midpoint formula obtained?\r\n4. Why do we take the absolute value in area?\r\n5. How can coordinate geometry test collinearity?\r\n\r\n## 13) mini quiz with answers\r\n\r\n### quiz\r\n1. Distance between two points is found using:\r\n   - a) AP formula\r\n   - b) Pythagoras-based formula\r\n   - c) probability rule\r\n   - d) tangent theorem\r\n\r\n2. Midpoint divides a segment in the ratio:\r\n   - a) `1:2`\r\n   - b) `2:1`\r\n   - c) `1:1`\r\n   - d) `0:1`\r\n\r\n3. If area of a triangle by coordinates is zero, the points are:\r\n   - a) equal\r\n   - b) collinear\r\n   - c) random\r\n   - d) opposite\r\n\r\n4. Midpoint of `(0, 0)` and `(6, 4)` is:\r\n   - a) `(3, 2)`\r\n   - b) `(6, 4)`\r\n   - c) `(2, 3)`\r\n   - d) `(4, 6)`\r\n\r\n5. In the distance formula, the coordinate differences are:\r\n   - a) added directly\r\n   - b) squared first\r\n   - c) multiplied only\r\n   - d) ignored\r\n\r\n### answer key\r\n1. b\r\n2. c\r\n3. b\r\n4. a\r\n5. b\r\n\r\n## 14) content style note for future lessons\r\n\r\nThis chapter works best with graph visuals and small substitution drills:\r\n- one lesson on distance\r\n- one lesson on section and midpoint\r\n- one lesson on area and collinearity\r\n\r\nStudents need plenty of sign-handling practice with negative coordinates.\r\n\r\n## comic style recap\r\n\r\nPanel 1: Coordinate Captain marks `(x, y)`: \"First across, then up or down.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 2: Distance Detective squares the differences: \"Subtraction first, square next.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 3: Midpoint Mechanic averages coordinates: \"Halfway means average.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 4: Area Inspector checks three points: \"Zero area means collinear.\"\r\n",
