    "markdown": "---\r\ntitle: Statistics\r\naudience: CBSE Class 10\r\nsubject: Mathematics\r\nchapter_number: 13\r\nchapter_slug: statistics\r\nstatus: draft\r\ncontent_type: chapter-pack\r\ntype: ChapterPack\r\ndescription: Class 10 CBSE notes for Statistics.\r\ntags:\r\n- cbse\r\n- class-10\r\n- statistics\r\ntimestamp: '2026-07-08T12:00:00Z'\r\n---\r\n# Statistics\r\n\r\nThis is an original teaching draft for Class 10 Mathematics, Chapter 13. The notes keep the focus on reading tables calmly and using the correct formula for grouped data.\r\n\r\n## chapter goal\r\n\r\nBy the end of this chapter, a student should be able to:\r\n- understand grouped frequency distribution\r\n- find the mean of grouped data\r\n- find the median of grouped data\r\n- find the mode of grouped data\r\n- read cumulative frequency and use it in median questions\r\n\r\n## teacher note on textbook alignment\r\n\r\nThe current NCERT Class 10 chapter strongly focuses on:\r\n- mean of grouped data\r\n- median of grouped data\r\n- mode of grouped data\r\n- cumulative frequency idea\r\n- practical table-based computation\r\n\r\n## chapter flow for the platform\r\n\r\n1. Frequency table basics\r\n2. Mean of grouped data\r\n3. Median of grouped data\r\n4. Mode of grouped data\r\n5. Cumulative frequency and median class\r\n6. Quick revision and practice\r\n\r\n## 1) grouped data basics\r\n\r\nIn grouped data, values are arranged in class intervals with frequencies.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n- `0-10`\r\n- `10-20`\r\n- `20-30`\r\n\r\nThe number of observations in each class is its frequency.\r\n\r\n### class mark\r\nClass mark is the midpoint of a class interval.\r\n\r\nFor `10-20`, class mark is:\r\n\r\n`(10 + 20)/2 = 15`\r\n\r\n## 2) mean of grouped data\r\n\r\nThe mean is the average.\r\n\r\nFor grouped data:\r\n\r\n`Mean = (sum f_i x_i) / (sum f_i)`\r\n\r\nwhere:\r\n- `f_i` = frequency\r\n- `x_i` = class mark\r\n\r\n### step-deviation method\r\nNCERT also uses assumed mean and step-deviation to make calculation easier.\r\n\r\nStudents should know the direct idea first and then the shortcut method.\r\n\r\n## 3) median of grouped data\r\n\r\nMedian is the middle value.\r\n\r\nFor grouped data:\r\n\r\n`Median = l + [(n/2 - cf) / f] x h`\r\n\r\nwhere:\r\n- `l` = lower boundary of median class\r\n- `n` = total frequency\r\n- `cf` = cumulative frequency before median class\r\n- `f` = frequency of median class\r\n- `h` = class size\r\n\r\n### key step\r\nFirst identify the median class using `n/2`.\r\n\r\n## 4) mode of grouped data\r\n\r\nMode is the value with the highest frequency.\r\n\r\nFor grouped data:\r\n\r\n`Mode = l + [(f1 - f0) / (2f1 - f0 - f2)] x h`\r\n\r\nwhere:\r\n- `l` = lower boundary of modal class\r\n- `f1` = frequency of modal class\r\n- `f0` = frequency before modal class\r\n- `f2` = frequency after modal class\r\n- `h` = class size\r\n\r\n### key step\r\nModal class is the class interval with highest frequency.\r\n\r\n## 5) cumulative frequency\r\n\r\nCumulative frequency means running total of frequencies.\r\n\r\nIt is especially useful for median questions.\r\n\r\n### example idea\r\nIf frequencies are `3, 5, 7`, cumulative frequencies are:\r\n`3, 8, 15`\r\n\r\nThis helps us locate where `n/2` lies.\r\n\r\n## 6) exam-friendly summary notes\r\n\r\n### must-know facts\r\n- Mean uses class marks.\r\n- Median needs cumulative frequency.\r\n- Mode depends on the modal class.\r\n- Median class is found using `n/2`.\r\n- Correct table work matters more than speed.\r\n\r\n### one-line revision points\r\n- mean = weighted average\r\n- median = middle class method\r\n- mode = highest-frequency class\r\n\r\n## 7) common mistakes students make\r\n\r\n1. Using class interval end points instead of class marks for mean.\r\n2. Forgetting to form cumulative frequency for median.\r\n3. Choosing the wrong median class.\r\n4. Mixing `f0`, `f1`, and `f2` in mode formula.\r\n5. Using wrong class size `h`.\r\n\r\n## 8) board tips\r\n\r\n- Make a neat table before calculation.\r\n- Show class marks and cumulative frequencies clearly.\r\n- In median questions, write `n/2` separately.\r\n- In mode questions, identify the modal class before substituting.\r\n\r\n## 9) how to remember the chapter\r\n\r\n### memory box 1\r\nMean uses middle points of classes.\r\n\r\n### memory box 2\r\nMedian is about location, not just averaging.\r\n\r\n### memory box 3\r\nMode means most frequent class.\r\n\r\n### memory box 4\r\nA neat table saves marks.\r\n\r\n## 10) solved practice set\r\n\r\n### question 1\r\nWhat is the class mark of `20-30`?\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\n`(20 + 30)/2 = 25`\r\n\r\n### question 2\r\nWhy is cumulative frequency useful?\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\nIt helps identify the median class.\r\n\r\n### question 3\r\nWhat is the modal class?\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\nThe class interval with the highest frequency.\r\n\r\n## 11) short answer practice\r\n\r\n1. What is grouped data?\r\n2. How do you find class mark?\r\n3. Why is `n/2` used in median?\r\n4. What is the modal class?\r\n5. Why should table work be neat in statistics?\r\n\r\n## 12) mini quiz with answers\r\n\r\n### quiz\r\n1. Mean of grouped data uses:\r\n   - a) class marks\r\n   - b) only lower limits\r\n   - c) only upper limits\r\n   - d) only frequency sum\r\n\r\n2. Median class is found using:\r\n   - a) `n`\r\n   - b) `n/2`\r\n   - c) `2n`\r\n   - d) `f1`\r\n\r\n3. Mode is linked to:\r\n   - a) smallest frequency\r\n   - b) equal frequencies\r\n   - c) highest frequency\r\n   - d) class mark only\r\n\r\n4. Running total of frequencies is:\r\n   - a) class size\r\n   - b) class mark\r\n   - c) cumulative frequency\r\n   - d) assumed mean\r\n\r\n5. Class mark of `30-40` is:\r\n   - a) `30`\r\n   - b) `35`\r\n   - c) `40`\r\n   - d) `70`\r\n\r\n### answer key\r\n1. a\r\n2. b\r\n3. c\r\n4. c\r\n5. b\r\n\r\n## 13) content style note for future lessons\r\n\r\nThis chapter should become table-first lessons:\r\n- mean with direct and assumed mean method\r\n- median with cumulative frequency\r\n- mode with modal class\r\n\r\nStudents need clean table habits and repeated substitution practice.\r\n\r\n## comic style recap\r\n\r\nPanel 1: Data Doctor arranges the table: \"Clean columns prevent wrong answers.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 2: Mean Machine balances the data: \"Average tells the centre by total.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 3: Median Mapper follows cumulative frequency: \"Find the middle position.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 4: Mode Monitor spots the modal class: \"Highest frequency gets attention.\"\r\n",
